<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
  <!-- This tells the browser what character set to use -->
  <meta charset="UTF-8">

  <!-- This helps the page fit correctly on phones and smaller screens -->
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <!-- This title shows in the browser tab -->
  <title>Programming Languages</title>

  <!-- This connects the page to our external CSS file -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/styles.css">
</head>

<body>

  <!-- Adds the shared header and navigation menu -->
  <%- include("partials/header.ejs") %>

  <!-- Main content for the home page -->
  <main>
    <section class="intro">
      <h2>What Are Programming Languages?</h2>

      <p>
        Programming languages are used to give instructions to computers.
        They help people create websites, apps, games, operating systems,
        and many other types of software.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each language has its own style, rules, and common uses.
        This website gives a simple overview of Python, Java, and C++.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Figure keeps the image and caption together -->
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/img/mainCoding.jpg"
        alt="Computer screens showing programming code"
      >

      <figcaption>
        Programming languages help people write instructions for computers.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h2>Why Programming Languages Matter</h2>

      <p>
        Programming languages allow people to solve problems using technology.
        A programmer can build websites, organize data, create apps, and control
        how devices work.
      </p>

      <p>
        Different languages are useful for different jobs. Python is often used
        for automation and data, Java is common in business software, and C++ is
        used when speed and control are important.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>What Is New in HW 4?</h2>

      <p>
        This Express version of HW 1 uses EJS views, reusable partials,
        data from a Node package, and live information from the GitHub API.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <!-- Adds the shared footer -->
  <%- include("partials/footer.ejs") %>

</body>

</html>